Bug summary: In Commons Math's AbstractIntegrator event handling (ODE integration), when one event handler returns RESET_STATE, the method returns early and the other event handlers that did not trigger in that step are not updated (reinitialized) to the new state/time. On the next step they still reference the old start state/date, so their g(t, y) functions get completely wrong y values (e.g. 1.0e20 instead of within [-1, 1]).

Concrete examples:
[{'input': "ODE integrator with multiple EventHandlers (e.g. two events); at a step, handler A's eventOccurred returns Action.RESET_STATE while handler B has no event in that step. Integration continues with the reset state.", 'expected': "After the reset, all event states (including handler B's) are updated to the new step/state, so g(t, y) is always called with consistent, correct y values (within -1..+1).", 'actual': "Due to an early return upon RESET_STATE, handler B's event state is not updated and still refers to the previous state's start date; on the next step g(t, y) is called with wrong y values (up to 1.0e20 when they should be in [-1, 1])."}]

